# Line endings
* text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Parquet: binary, merged as a set of readings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The store is committed, and both the hourly job and a local transform or
# backfill write the current month's partition. Git can't merge binary
# content, so every divergence used to stop `git pull --rebase` for a hand
# resolution.
#
# A partition is an append-only set of readings keyed by record id, site
# and variable, so merging two versions has a well-defined answer: keep
# every reading either side has, once — three-way against the common
# ancestor, so a purge or a repair on one side survives. That's
# src/secchi/merge_parquet.py, registered per clone by:
#
#     pixi run setup-git
#
# A clone WITHOUT that setup falls back to the old behaviour: git reports
# the conflict and leaves a readable file. Verified — it does not attempt
# a text merge on the binary. So an unconfigured machine is no worse off.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: later lines win. `binary` expands to -diff -merge -text,
# so the general rule must come BEFORE the specific one, or it would
# switch the driver back off. An earlier draft had them the other way
# round; `git check-attr merge -- <file>` is how to confirm.
*.parquet binary
data/processed/**/*.parquet merge=parquet-union

# Raw snapshots are JSON with unique filenames per run, so they append
# rather than conflict. Marked -diff only to keep `git log -p` readable.
data/raw/**/*.json -diff
